WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.436
(playful music)

00:00:01.436 --> 00:00:03.664
- [Narrator] Mm, donuts.

00:00:03.664 --> 00:00:06.421
The guiltiest of guilty pleasures.

00:00:06.421 --> 00:00:08.330
But where did the donut get its shape,

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.626
and more specifically, who
put the hole in the donut?

00:00:11.626 --> 00:00:13.237
Two competing theories set the stage

00:00:13.237 --> 00:00:16.677
for what would become known as
the Great Donut Debate.

00:00:16.677 --> 00:00:18.344
(jazzy music)

00:00:21.092 --> 00:00:23.281
- [Michael] If you think of
the quintessential American

00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:26.545
sweet snack, it is in fact the donut.

00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:28.150
- [Narrator] That's Michel Krondl.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:28.983
He wrote this book.

00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:31.452
- [Michael] The Donut,
History, Recipes, and Lore

00:00:31.452 --> 00:00:33.379
from Boston to Berlin.

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:35.312
- [Narrator] Suffice to
say Krondl is what you call

00:00:35.312 --> 00:00:36.662
a donut expert.

00:00:36.662 --> 00:00:38.467
- [Michael] When you have
something so central to a culture

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.374
as the donut is to American culture,

00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:42.449
you wanna tell stories around it.

00:00:42.449 --> 00:00:44.394
- [Narrator] We here at Great
Big Story are no exception.

00:00:44.394 --> 00:00:45.783
(classical music)

00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:48.190
First part of our story takes us to 1916,

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.648
where a Boston Post reporter interviewed

00:00:49.648 --> 00:00:51.472
Hanson Crockett Gregory.

00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:53.289
- [Michael] Gregory gives
this long interview,

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:55.107
where he explains how he invented it,

00:00:55.107 --> 00:00:57.129
which was that he was on board ship

00:00:57.129 --> 00:00:59.707
when he was around 15 years old

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.978
when he had the clever idea
of poking out the middle

00:01:02.978 --> 00:01:05.990
so that the whole thing would cook evenly.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.157
This, he claims, made him the
Columbus of the donut hole.

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.435
- [Narrator] Gregory's
story spread like wildfire.

00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:14.836
- [Michael] The story was
picked up by newspapers

00:01:14.836 --> 00:01:15.880
all over the country.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.630
This made him a star.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.993
It was little unclear why they believed

00:01:20.993 --> 00:01:24.202
the story of an 85-year-old sailor,

00:01:24.202 --> 00:01:26.268
given the reputations sailors have.

00:01:26.268 --> 00:01:27.829
- [Narrator] Skepticism
aside, donuts grew

00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:29.275
more and more popular over the years,

00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:32.570
due in large part to a
donut dunking fad.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.317
- [Michael] Donut dunking
becomes a national obsession.

00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:40.311
- [Narrator] In 1941, the
National Dunking Association,

00:01:40.311 --> 00:01:43.771
yes, a real thing, hosted
the Great Donut Debate,

00:01:43.771 --> 00:01:47.938
where the question was "Who
put the hole in the donut?"

00:01:50.131 --> 00:01:53.708
- [Michael] And one side of
the debate was the proposal

00:01:53.708 --> 00:01:56.803
that Captain Gregory had in
fact invented the donut hole

00:01:56.803 --> 00:02:00.349
as he had explained, which
is as a youth on the ship.

00:02:00.349 --> 00:02:02.204
- [Narrator] And on the
other side, Chief High Eagle

00:02:02.204 --> 00:02:03.786
of Wampanoag Tribe.

00:02:03.786 --> 00:02:04.619
- [Michael] Chief High Eagle

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:06.481
had a completely different story.

00:02:06.481 --> 00:02:09.647
Many generations ago,
a member of his tribe

00:02:09.647 --> 00:02:11.564
was pursuing a settler.

00:02:12.943 --> 00:02:16.446
And this settler apparently
had a donut in her hand,

00:02:16.446 --> 00:02:19.887
and this young Indian brave shot an arrow,

00:02:19.887 --> 00:02:23.330
which created a hole right in
the middle of the donut.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.649
- [Narrator] In the
end, the celebrity panel

00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:27.131
awarded Gregory with the innovation.

00:02:27.131 --> 00:02:28.994
And though we can never prove
that Hanson Crockett Gregory

00:02:28.994 --> 00:02:30.831
put the first hole in the donut,

00:02:30.831 --> 00:02:32.476
the tale lives on.

00:02:32.476 --> 00:02:35.143
(playful music)

